Boxing is the process of converting a value type to the type object or to any interface type implemented by this value type. 
When the common language runtime (CLR) boxes a value type, it wraps the value inside a System.Object instance 
and stores it on the managed heap. Unboxing extracts the value type from the object. 
Boxing is implicit; unboxing is explicit. 
The concept of boxing and unboxing underlies the C# unified view of the type system 
in which a value of any type can be treated as an object.

Avoid boxing by using generic collections (List<T>, Dictionary<TKey,TValue>) instead of non-generic collections (ArrayList, Hashtable).

Boxing converts a value type into an object, which requires allocating a new object on the heap and copying the value. 
This increases memory allocations and GC pressure. 
In high-performance systems where operations occur millions of times, excessive boxing can significantly degrade performance
